{"id":"masked-diffusion-lm-loss","t":"Masked diffusion language-model loss","tags":["Language Models","Diffusion Models"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"masked_diffusion_loss","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"A masked diffusion language model (as in LLaDA) corrupts a sequence by replacing each token with `[MASK]` independently with probability $t$, for a noise level $t$ drawn uniformly from $(0, 1]$, and trains a bidirectional Transformer to predict the original tokens at the masked positions. The loss is weighted by $1/t$, which makes it an upper bound on the negative log-likelihood.\n\nWrite `masked_diffusion_loss(x0, t, u, logits, mask_id, prompt_len=None)` returning `(xt, loss)`.\n\n## Inputs\n- `x0`: `(B, L)` int clean tokens. `t`: `(B,)` noise levels in $(0, 1]$. `u`: `(B, L)` uniform draws in $[0, 1)$.\n- Position $i$ of row $b$ is masked when $u_{b,i} < t_b$. If `prompt_len` `(B,)` is given (supervised fine-tuning), positions $i <$ `prompt_len[b]` are never masked.\n- `logits`: `(B, L, V)`, the mask predictor's output on `xt` (supplied so you don't need a network).\n\n## Output\n- `xt`: `x0` with masked positions set to `mask_id`.\n- `loss` (float): the batch mean of\n  $$\\ell_b = \\frac{1}{t_b\\,N_b}\\sum_{i\\ \\text{masked}} -\\log \\mathrm{softmax}(\\text{logits}_{b,i})[x_{0,b,i}]$$\n  where $N_b = L$ in pre-training and $N_b = L - \\text{prompt\\_len}_b$ (the response length) in fine-tuning.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef masked_diffusion_loss(x0, t, u, logits, mask_id, prompt_len=None):\n    \"\"\"Return (xt, loss).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Build the boolean mask `u < t[:, None]` first, then remove the prompt positions with a position comparison against `prompt_len[:, None]`.","Gather the log-probability of the clean token at every position with `take_along_axis`, zero the unmasked ones, sum per row and divide by $t_b N_b$."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef masked_diffusion_loss(x0, t, u, logits, mask_id, prompt_len=None):\n    x0, t, u = np.asarray(x0), np.asarray(t, dtype=float), np.asarray(u, dtype=float)\n    B, L = x0.shape\n    masked = u < t[:, None]\n    if prompt_len is None:\n        N = np.full(B, L, dtype=float)\n    else:\n        pl = np.asarray(prompt_len)\n        masked &= np.arange(L)[None, :] >= pl[:, None]\n        N = (L - pl).astype(float)\n    xt = np.where(masked, mask_id, x0)\n    z = np.asarray(logits, dtype=float)\n    z = z - z.max(-1, keepdims=True)\n    logp = z - np.log(np.exp(z).sum(-1, keepdims=True))\n    nll = -np.take_along_axis(logp, x0[..., None], axis=-1)[..., 0]\n    per_row = (nll * masked).sum(1) / (t * N)\n    return xt, float(per_row.mean())\n","ex_md":"## Why the $1/t$ weight\nAt noise level $t$ about $tL$ tokens are masked, so the raw sum of their losses grows with $t$. Dividing by $t$ makes every noise level contribute on the same per-token scale, and integrating the weighted loss over $t \\sim U(0,1]$ gives an upper bound on $-\\log p(x_0)$, which is what makes this a proper likelihood-based generative model rather than BERT with a random mask rate.\n\n## Compared with BERT's MLM\nBERT masks a fixed 15% and never generates. A masked diffusion model sees every mask rate from almost none to all, so at sampling time it can start from a fully masked sequence and unmask it over several steps.\n\n## Fine-tuning\nOnly the response is ever masked; the prompt stays visible and acts as the condition. Normalising by response length keeps short and long answers on the same scale.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(BLV)$ for the log-softmax, as for any language-model loss.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Normalising by the number of masked tokens instead of $tL$ (that is a different, biased estimator).\n- Letting prompt tokens be masked in fine-tuning.\n- Including unmasked positions in the loss: the model sees them as input, so predicting them is trivial and distorts the objective.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Why is drawing $t$ from $(0, 1]$ rather than $[0, 1]$ important for this loss?\n- How does sampling from such a model work, and why can it unmask several tokens per step?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[4, 4, 4, 3, 5],\n       [4, 3, 0, 2, 0],\n       [1, 2, 4, 5, 5]])\narray([0.5, 0.5, 0.5])\narray([[0.14877082, 0.6853886 , 0.17275845, 0.96737549, 0.59006599],\n       [0.70280543, 0.94606808, 0.4358881 , 0.87123927, 0.89721486],\n       [0.74351555, 0.00985276, 0.9830191 , 0.26235771, 0.13617844]])\narray([[[-1.09421662,  0.51219086,  0.24832981, -0.71977505,\n          0.56261187,  0.19152376,  0.86158547],\n        [-0.14951966, -0.07671297, -1.0874201 ,  0.81830519,\n         -0.19398004, -0.3633484 ,  0.2195938 ],\n        [-1.27508928, -0.36211295, -0.98629912,  0.28351922,\n          0.61148772, -0.10524717,  2.00224225],\n        [-0.1759712 ,  0.54118842, -0.81834011, -0.09045562,\n         -1.15738738, -0.62841039, -0.96188844],\n        [ 1.07181605,  0.39231167,  1.14128908, -0.06976116,\n         -2.00690421, -0.75976817, -0.04612839]],\n\n       [[-0.05205887,  1.43229613, -0.22935489,  …\n6","out":"(array([[6, 4, 6, 3, 5],\n       [4, 3, 6, 2, 0],\n       [1, 6, 4, 6, 6]]), 1.9337490825069439)"},{"in":"array([[4, 4, 4, 3, 5],\n       [4, 3, 0, 2, 0],\n       [1, 2, 4, 5, 5]])\narray([1.  , 0.2 , 0.05])\narray([[0.82544258, 0.20778224, 0.60665468, 0.61832595, 0.16567135],\n       [0.1190409 , 0.9885131 , 0.89179794, 0.0136704 , 0.53325685],\n       [0.94882194, 0.47358034, 0.25237966, 0.80181892, 0.45861377]])\narray([[[-1.09421662,  0.51219086,  0.24832981, -0.71977505,\n          0.56261187,  0.19152376,  0.86158547],\n        [-0.14951966, -0.07671297, -1.0874201 ,  0.81830519,\n         -0.19398004, -0.3633484 ,  0.2195938 ],\n        [-1.27508928, -0.36211295, -0.98629912,  0.28351922,\n          0.61148772, -0.10524717,  2.00224225],\n        [-0.1759712 ,  0.54118842, -0.81834011, -0.09045562,\n         -1.15738738, -0.62841039, -0.96188844],\n        [ 1.07181605,  0.39231167,  1.14128908, -0.0697